#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055697} :activity/id #uuid "cade22aa-6331-58b6-892c-984d4c3bae76" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048548 :topic/id #uuid "a8f3106d-906d-52d1-b148-e532348d89d2" :topic/math-academy-id 79} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055997 :tutorial/content "Polynomials with one, two, or three terms have special names.\n\n- A polynomial with one term is called a **monomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\n1, \\qquad y,\t\\qquad\t2x^3,\t\\qquad\t5a^{4}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following expressions are not monomials because they can't be called polynomials:\n \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y},\t\\qquad\t5t^{-1},\t\\qquad \\sqrt{x}, \\qquad \t2z^{1/4}\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with two terms is called a **binomial**. For example:\n\n$$\nx+3,\t\\qquad\t7n^2 +n,\t\\qquad\tp^{32} -1\n$$\n\n- A polynomial with three terms is called a **trinomial**. For example:\n \n\n$$\nz^2 -z +1,\t\\qquad\tx^3 -2x -1,\t\\qquad\t2c^{4} - 3c^{3} +4c^2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e82b56d-3e2d-582a-b73f-3c5c98830a58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6660 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d5ad5bf3-9168-540f-8492-ce7c9fc4b988" :step/math-academy-id 40308 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054348 :question/id #uuid "b8742776-1dc7-5816-afb6-9a72142ecb5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3a71018-8f67-53cb-aa6c-7f17e0af9ef9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050306}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475967}] :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by the Number of Terms It Has"} :step/id #uuid "38cccf7d-917a-5a79-aa78-f1a2b54e1a10" :step/math-academy-id 40309 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055998 :tutorial/content "Each type of polynomial of degree between $0$ and $5$ has a specific name. These are shown in the table below.\n\n| Degree | Name | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | Constant | $7$ |\n| $1$ | Linear | $3x + 2$ |\n| $2$ | Quadratic | $2x^2 - 6x + 1$ |\n| $3$ | Cubic | $x^3 + 4x^2 + 9x$ |\n| $4$ | Quartic | $3x^4 - 5x^2$ |\n| $5$ | Quintic | $7x^5 + 3x^4 - x^4 + x - 6$ |\n\nWe can combine these with the names we learned earlier. \n\nFor example:\n\n- $2x+1$ is a *linear binomial* because its degree equals $1,$ and it has $2$ terms.\n\n- $x^2$ is a *quadratic monomial* because its degree equals $2,$ and it has $1$ term.\n\n- $2x^3+x+1$ is a *cubic trinomial* because its degree equals $3,$ and it has $3$ terms.\n\nIf a polynomial contains more than three terms, then we refer to it by its degree only.\n\nFor example:\n\n- $x^3+2x^2+x-1$ is a *cubic polynomial.*\n\n- $x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1$ is a *quartic polynomial.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "ed2578f7-b1e4-5f83-b963-d66d7b97fa9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6661 :tutorial/title "Names of Polynomials Based on Their Degree"} :step/id #uuid "ac189d1f-bdee-52ff-86b2-d371b3d6e1c6" :step/math-academy-id 40312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054349 :question/id #uuid "6270e77f-6ccb-5304-90da-2419fa309007" :question/math-academy-id "e-10651"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b8feb96-7432-5fe7-a6c7-b2d8ebe7e766" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048406}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533990}] :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by Its Degree and Number of Terms"} :step/id #uuid "08e0cbe9-22f1-5b66-a44f-4af1547552f5" :step/math-academy-id 40313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055701}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054350 :question/id #uuid "3400928d-9463-56f2-af47-e193a1e18a47" :question/math-academy-id "e-10652"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd2d5a4-73de-51ad-9f9f-070d1d4f31a5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048524}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553577}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"} :step/id #uuid "227abef4-c6c1-5c73-9556-5ca01a686b40" :step/math-academy-id 40316}] :activity/title "Monomials, Binomials and Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054924}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493977} :activity/id #uuid "9d9c15c2-a381-503f-8c20-095ae4a1ed50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048548 :topic/id #uuid "a8f3106d-906d-52d1-b148-e532348d89d2" :topic/math-academy-id 79} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1056a69e-6f45-5662-8064-9d31764878d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Quadratic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493420} {:answer/id #uuid "683a7cea-5988-5760-91b7-9df2a330db1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Quartic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493546} {:answer/id #uuid "71053b83-643d-5ef2-8a38-901d38fcd2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Quartic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493557} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee88caf-fa42-587f-9b4e-37e2a9c5dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Cubic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493617} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c26456-8a47-5d01-856e-a94ad2152272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Cubic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493686} :answer-field/id #uuid "61ac3cf1-ce85-58c6-8c68-c0b075726ad0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "becc645b-7ce0-5458-abaa-ac04d1cbd85e" :question/math-academy-id "q-51576" :question/problem "Classify the polynomial $5{y}^{3}-2y+1$ according to its degree and the number of terms." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $5{y}^{3}-2y+1$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $3,$ so it is a cubic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a cubic trinomial."} :step/id #uuid "05338efe-276c-51da-8655-6d896721903f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61b11b3a-81a6-51e7-8ad5-0ea10ae35041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493537} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3ca694-1f66-5b36-b822-4d155549fa8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493585} {:answer/id #uuid "a66f3f1f-29ec-5be2-8bc5-722c8e836b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493654} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b1e4de-0805-5bfa-8e45-f45979420ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493753} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a93d32-18e7-5f8e-961d-886b53773bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493792}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493537} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a727332-6125-5bf9-8eee-55ebc8cbefb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a6f2377-d91b-5967-99d3-f6f62abbce5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-152221" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The sum of $4{x}^{2}+1$ and $2{x}^{2}$ is a binomial.\n\nII. The sum of $3{x}^{2}-2x$ and $5$ is a binomial.\n\nIII. The sum of a binomial and a monomial is **sometimes** a binomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n(4{x}^{2}+1)+(2{x}^{2})=6{x}^{2}+1,\n$$\n\nwhich is a binomial.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n(3{x}^{2}-2x)+(5)=3{x}^{2}-2x+5,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a binomial (it's a trinomial).\n\n- Statement III is true. The sum of a binomial and a monomial *could be* a binomial (see, for example, statement I). \n\n However, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is *not always* a binomial (see, for example, statement II). \n\n So, the sum of a binomial and a monomial is *sometimes* binomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "33040ec4-d764-5460-b879-febde2296a30"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d6c22d6-e892-5446-9098-76a0b21515ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "linear binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493442} {:answer/id #uuid "32c65879-0672-5962-bea0-b0afdace1faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "quadratic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493477} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6cf1b6-04ad-59dd-b257-00f1e1dce62c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "quintic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493489} {:answer/id #uuid "44e3a524-e613-5db6-91fb-801225462723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "cubic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493506} {:answer/id #uuid "8caae5ad-2091-5a94-9de8-16bbe05ea834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "quartic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493489} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfb5c23-969b-59ed-8fd1-dd67557eb34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6b20681-3c0e-56d8-a0d2-12a0033bb72d" :question/math-academy-id "q-111223" :question/problem "Categorize the polynomial ${z}^{5}-3z$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial ${z}^{5}-3z$ has two terms, so it is a binomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $5,$ so it is a quintic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quintic binomial."} :step/id #uuid "39e3b333-15cb-5985-99ff-5688251c0604" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493992}}] :activity/title "Monomials, Binomials and Trinomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493384}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060470} :activity/id #uuid "8089f422-4708-55f1-8234-e77b87f79d18" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048737 :topic/id #uuid "b9ffda55-6967-5939-88c6-796a478b7b95" :topic/math-academy-id 262} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060470 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060874 :tutorial/content "Let's consider an equilateral triangle with sides of length $2x$. Notice that any altitude will split it into two congruent triangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nIn the new smaller triangle the acute angles will be $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ.$ Such triangles are called **$\\boldsymbol{30^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{60^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\nBased on the picture above, we see a special property shared by all $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles:\n\n*In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nThis also works in the other direction:\n\n*If the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg, then the given right triangle is a ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "e07c8310-fe8a-5f3e-9ff0-7cd4e93a784c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2383 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f3aaec85-3c9b-585d-9138-8646ce0db776" :step/math-academy-id 10425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060471}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060471 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059637 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059465 :question/id #uuid "b5f19406-37c2-569e-9c9e-b99e00234f91" :question/math-academy-id "e-3084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cc0e4fc-64ca-5c1b-abbe-357e930af7a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491201}] :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Length of the Hypotenuse to the Length Of the Shortest Side"} :step/id #uuid "a6377883-059a-5ebf-97b1-38ef2ad683fd" :step/math-academy-id 10427 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060875 :tutorial/content "Consider the same ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle from before. This time, however, we will focus on the height ($h$) instead of the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nApplying the Pythagorean Theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{h}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{3}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the second important property:\n\n*In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg (which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle) is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the length of the shorter leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nWe can summarize everything we know about ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangles using the diagram below:\n\n![](images/85/8598f9163b29833bb5895416e3dec9b6aa2358bd2cfcca944625ebb9f576b3a4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2dbb6c4-dedc-56fa-8e66-87e843a7f7fb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2386 :tutorial/title "The Longer Leg"} :step/id #uuid "860a330d-51b9-5aba-a7ce-25da9f5b8254" :step/math-academy-id 10429 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060474}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059639 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059467 :question/id #uuid "f399fe29-f767-5ea5-bc2b-0d47c0e8fbaf" :question/math-academy-id "e-3086"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26db86bd-122c-5d1b-9684-9444897d1c44" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498594}] :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Length of the Shorter Leg to the Length of the Longer Leg"} :step/id #uuid "ec4f5b67-5c3e-5ce2-bb3a-7b6b95c2a0c8" :step/math-academy-id 10432 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059640 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059468 :question/id #uuid "40a94955-95e0-5945-9a84-d51b8c691bce" :question/math-academy-id "e-3088"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca6bd1cf-98e8-5fc8-b605-dd4d24ac8f6c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491188}] :knowledge-point/title "Relating the Length of the Hypotenuse to the Length Of the Longer Leg"} :step/id #uuid "ab1f256a-5f6e-5d12-9b0f-482675761ab1" :step/math-academy-id 10434}] :activity/title "30-60-90 Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059937}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498606} :activity/id #uuid "ec4d6c60-9493-5c8e-8def-a6cecde39a5a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048737 :topic/id #uuid "b9ffda55-6967-5939-88c6-796a478b7b95" :topic/math-academy-id 262} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c1eea0-7c1a-5512-a40b-b00ac6438521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497928} {:answer/id #uuid "72d18ca3-fe51-56a9-9bfa-889608a412a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498072} {:answer/id #uuid "846d4048-65cc-5295-b258-ea3a58ef9c5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498105} {:answer/id #uuid "c2a38599-4447-5901-bca9-9207e3d7d95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498229} {:answer/id #uuid "e911db0b-16ef-5b6b-9539-32d02e521317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498072} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdbaebde-f52c-5ca0-b6d8-fe1dfd1cd8d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c6bb31a7-2bac-5692-829b-abedff759b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-53474" :question/problem "![](images/56/56b286d9b7c503c84891ebdf410797f715844eb8de344f71dc2fc652cbf3b5ef.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, what is the length of $\\overset{―}{AC}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's refer to our standard ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](images/85/85f119cdc6b7a80dd7cd4ea2a2a235e80723b7c2fe6837fa127beb8fd994d7ea.png)\n\nSince this is a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the shorter leg has length $x=3$ and the longer leg has length $\\sqrt{3}x,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC=\\sqrt{3}x=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 3=3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the longer leg is $3\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "095a68ee-12f6-53d3-a8d2-90e37f449112" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e34be6-9d43-52e0-b22e-907ce34bc24d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497870} {:answer/id #uuid "73884abd-8071-5124-a2c0-7bf649d6ebc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498075} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3833b4-dcec-536d-bf70-19da49848ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498155} {:answer/id #uuid "bdba409b-c32c-5c89-936d-51658a2ee188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498220} {:answer/id #uuid "f895f4a1-8bdc-521b-b919-11a1fec165de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498075} :answer-field/id #uuid "8711fdf9-4038-5d22-903a-76bcdd15aa01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e7ef9681-e380-5003-b606-ef9960f3bfee" :question/math-academy-id "q-119569" :question/problem "What is the length of the side opposite the ${30}^{∘}$ angle in a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle if the side opposite the ${60}^{∘}$ angle has a length of $21?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's refer to our standard ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](images/1f/1f7084b210e13a9c2753c3ae8bfcc9302b109cc9584f589c6b8937536ae93682.png)\n\n  \n\nSince this is a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the longer leg has length $21,$ and the shorter leg has length $x,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =\\sqrt{3}x \\\\ x & =\\frac{21}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the radical from the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and denominator by the radical:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{21}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{21\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{21\\sqrt{3}}{3}=7\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the shorter leg is $7\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "45a49306-50df-53e5-a942-01eb1a2f5ffd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4da9fb6c-fce4-5acb-9e17-120df5e26665" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497983} {:answer/id #uuid "6a18641a-fc11-59e5-854c-6411d7507876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498056} {:answer/id #uuid "6a325992-e856-576c-91f7-df3f2ff65284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498057} {:answer/id #uuid "72d672f8-30a6-5729-819b-bcd0ca845735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498073} {:answer/id #uuid "78288c7d-e32f-51d9-91dc-975f09e50567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498084}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498084} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b57fc3-f626-5cfd-921b-551fd91eff75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "66769d5d-040d-51bf-90cd-9a689f5af1ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-119567" :question/problem "![](images/42/4234a330c5f497e3eae5af5006b87197f6911114f8b104b9050223d08ae7d310.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, what is the length of the shorter leg?" :question/worked-solution "Let's refer to our standard ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](images/b8/b8f2ba89d30a41072f635916eb07b394fd4bd83eb39181f13e852d1b776de5ee.png)\n\nSince this is a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the longer leg has length $7\\sqrt{3},$ and the shorter leg has length $x,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}x \\\\ x & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the shorter leg is $7.$"} :step/id #uuid "91dbe6b8-3a0b-5bb5-abce-3fde9a626f55" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498624}}] :activity/title "30-60-90 Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497797}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084066} :activity/id #uuid "654c7bcb-8774-5d52-8516-2b544db9deef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048891 :topic/id #uuid "c9c9a17a-1e46-5b3e-873e-a9145f5c883d" :topic/math-academy-id 698} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084516 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x}=\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhen the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nBefore we start, though, we note that $x=0$ *cannot* be a solution. This is because when $x=0$, we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side.\n\nNow, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =x\\cdot 3 \\\\ 10 & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can solve for $x$ using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x= \\dfrac{10}{3}$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x = \\dfrac{10}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "89ea4830-364e-5105-8df0-54cf9f7e1e36" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8779 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc5abf0d-f453-5c16-a078-3ade2b5464f0" :step/math-academy-id 170984 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084067}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082936 :question/id #uuid "5d89b84d-71cc-5c63-96f1-8abc8e2b2ca9" :question/math-academy-id "e-14140"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "783c067b-9676-57f1-81a9-7724c18e1534" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492067}] :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations With Variables in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "a296ae16-d9ac-5336-88fb-84c518347b2a" :step/math-academy-id 170985 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084517 :tutorial/content "If there is a negative sign in a rational equation, we can still solve it using cross-multiplication. The only difference is that we have to move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction first.\n\nFor example, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = -\\dfrac 9 8 .\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the equation from the previous example, but this time, there is a negative sign on the right-hand side.\n\nWe deal with the negative sign by moving it onto the numerator of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed to solve the equation by cross-multiplication, as usual. (We also note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.) Cross-multiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} \\[5pt]\n\t\t3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot (-9) \\[5pt]\n\t\t24 &= -18x\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t24 &= -18x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{24}{-18} &= \\dfrac{-18x}{-18} \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{4}{3} &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = -\\dfrac{4}{3}$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d61fcf8b-f639-5c4c-8997-66813230bedf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8780 :tutorial/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs"} :step/id #uuid "ccd40655-e59d-59b1-925d-4907d84cca52" :step/math-academy-id 170988 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082937 :question/id #uuid "4a26be93-e244-596b-9341-383dbbbc3e88" :question/math-academy-id "e-14142"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20c12caf-9f3e-5dcb-a12c-5c368824ede9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553564}] :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs"} :step/id #uuid "5cdcec66-242d-5007-990f-d0802020baee" :step/math-academy-id 170989 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083194 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082938 :question/id #uuid "cbd8760f-af04-5196-90d6-0d4cfdfaec2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14145"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8f96a06-1ebb-5ed1-9e47-23525e2a756e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491997}] :knowledge-point/title "Rational Equations With Binomials in One Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "1970d372-6794-5a8a-8180-36e7434e572b" :step/math-academy-id 170992}] :activity/title "Solving Rational Equations Using Cross-Multiplication" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083407}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106737} :activity/id #uuid "2fb56d40-545a-510d-a693-25bf992fdb77" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048921 :topic/id #uuid "cc9dbee5-9d12-52e7-a3e4-6d1b31e3ecaf" :topic/math-academy-id 1386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107044 :tutorial/content "Consider the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46a85010d46969ffb7df440efd72bb3fa8d1394b35cc2887ee4567cda15079c5.png)\n\nThe sum of the length of its sides is called the **perimeter**. Here, the perimeter of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\  & =2+4.2+3.8 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7664d7c3-e47c-57b6-97aa-1131f036852c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2244 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2dd79ef0-aa4a-5d52-ba67-92d8d47fd909" :step/math-academy-id 623181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106738}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105806 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105528 :question/id #uuid "00942425-ab13-5a50-a63a-d29cf2f34889" :question/math-academy-id "e-2752"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1d4b0c2-10d0-5c3a-8a58-4d7598dd9f96" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050410}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553559}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Perimeter of a Triangle Given the Perimeter of Another Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "48b5c886-bff4-52b2-b702-6be7c990ade1" :step/math-academy-id 623182 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107045 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a quadrilateral $ABCD$ as shown below and it is known that ${AD} = 6\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${AB} = 2\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${BC} = 4\\,\\mathrm{m},$ and $CD=5\\,\\mathrm{m}.$ What is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?   \n\n![](images/a7/a74251f1c72eccad42b98e0d3fd9a7e7cd0a60018a6f03bb65ec2abc34eb1c88.png)\n\nRemember that the **perimeter** $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AD+AB+BC+CD \\\\  & =6+2+4+5 \\\\  & =17\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0428807f-e6a6-5280-81bc-ab454203aebf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2246 :tutorial/title "Perimeters of Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "2154c4c2-efe1-5888-87e0-905e612555d4" :step/math-academy-id 623185 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106740}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105807 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105529 :question/id #uuid "b4864184-6983-58ab-9787-524e04dc5c73" :question/math-academy-id "e-2754"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f6c0ca1-297a-5f25-bccf-c74d0c565c24" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508083}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Perimeter of a Polygon"} :step/id #uuid "2896eab9-aca7-5163-8e08-2101c4fa2827" :step/math-academy-id 623186 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107046 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the polygons depicted below and find their perimeters.\n\n![](images/69/69ef06a71f13625ad4525a03966f4a4c62ae2b2136b085e97b8dc02a6a6963cc.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we conclude that polygons I, II and III all have a perimeter of $10$ while polygon IV has a perimeter of $8.$ \n\nPolygons that have the same perimeter are called **isoperimetric**. So I, II and III are isoperimetric polygons." :tutorial/id #uuid "596db00e-9cf7-51e1-902e-73c6670780eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2247 :tutorial/title "Isoperimetric Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "ffc5ce2a-4bcb-5660-a1ce-e79bd1b4c604" :step/math-academy-id 623189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105530 :question/id #uuid "3de76784-4137-5093-9c35-847edd10ef62" :question/math-academy-id "e-2756"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2c2a15f-617d-5515-98a8-ccdc525092b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492071}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of Variables Given Two Isoperimetric Polygons"} :step/id #uuid "ec3bbafa-69db-5676-8013-221157e979a6" :step/math-academy-id 623190}] :activity/title "The Perimeter of a Polygon" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106009}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111531} :activity/id #uuid "812cb5a9-d9bb-5df5-bcad-60b2e4bca49c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049059 :topic/id #uuid "d690f7fb-8ed3-54e1-86b0-fda3f8281846" :topic/math-academy-id 1457} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110616 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110506 :question/id #uuid "17173a8a-f28b-5456-8dbb-eb80d6d3cb1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4713"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd2dc211-25cb-5975-a665-f1f6ffd52488" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547022}] :knowledge-point/title "Combining Various Product and Quotient Rules of Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "e1f34a4c-5543-5841-9a26-631425dd8218" :step/math-academy-id 17217 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110507 :question/id #uuid "f145655c-ed98-578d-b7ab-7b18bfbfa107" :question/math-academy-id "e-4714"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba81d3-d555-5dc8-a904-f0f7e9520225" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050697}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549995}] :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Power Rule of Exponents With Other Rules"} :step/id #uuid "869d7516-5f01-5ae5-8aa5-fa957c225b74" :step/math-academy-id 17218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110618 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110508 :question/id #uuid "43141ab7-5ebf-5175-a512-e04f6646978d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4752"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "376ceac4-b0bb-5178-81e9-38e724cceaee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050668}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486448}] :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Product and Quotient Rules of Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "99b9d947-8b7b-5e8a-a9e4-9d2b3a2979a2" :step/math-academy-id 17336 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111884 :tutorial/content "Let's recap some of the rules for exponents.\n\n- **Product rule**: to multiply powers of the same base, add the exponents.\n\n- **Quotient rule**: to divide powers of the same base, subtract the exponents.\n\n- **Power rule**: to raise a power to another power, multiply the exponents.\n\n- **Power of product rule**: to raise the product of two numbers to a power, raise each number to that power, and then multiply.\n\n- **Power of quotient rule**: to raise a fraction to a power, raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power.\n\nWe can use a combination of these rules to simplify expressions containing exponents. Let's see an example of this." :tutorial/id #uuid "7c442391-b257-5c16-9fe0-292a45e3c172" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3275 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8150e1a1-0f41-5dc7-a1db-5e474331d638" :step/math-academy-id 17999 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111525}}] :activity/title "Combining the Rules of Exponents" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110829}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502198} :activity/id #uuid "890e7c6c-adee-5d25-828e-7834d97d256f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049059 :topic/id #uuid "d690f7fb-8ed3-54e1-86b0-fda3f8281846" :topic/math-academy-id 1457} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502168 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41468d21-4c10-5a19-948e-44ada7c0a123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{4}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501587} {:answer/id #uuid "7eba4019-7fbd-5be5-9b6f-4d896804f8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{6}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501704} {:answer/id #uuid "91298573-6a3f-5b5b-86e7-0b49355cfd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{4}^{12}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501742} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbea38b-7606-5fb6-b9c6-c107413a84d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{4}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501762} {:answer/id #uuid "d404240e-f93e-5c72-adcb-8512850b4fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{4}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501587} :answer-field/id #uuid "f75d50b2-6f5a-5bce-9e0b-522b9985c2e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4eaff0b-1470-58ee-9066-30ef9be58837" :question/math-academy-id "q-68964" :question/problem "${(\\frac{6}{{4}^{2}})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the power of quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{6}{{4}^{2}})}^{3}=\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{({4}^{2})}^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the denominator, we use the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{({4}^{2})}^{3}} & =\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{4}^{2\\cdot 3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{4}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "102a9be1-877b-502d-8f9a-b0b5673f06b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502173}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3611b83a-05fd-57c0-964f-6cdb6a750d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{6}^{8}\\cdot {8}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501565} {:answer/id #uuid "74a93e7b-8e23-5952-a163-17d5bb75323b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{6}^{3}\\cdot {8}^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501680} {:answer/id #uuid "a8011c4e-cb58-515e-9fe0-ab3c788f1478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{6}^{8}\\cdot {8}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501793} {:answer/id #uuid "cf81bb58-a87f-5a0e-91a5-9f5976da0085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{6}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501878} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f55899-6b14-567d-959e-9963101cb1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{6}^{3}\\cdot {8}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501793} :answer-field/id #uuid "11312e6f-7278-523b-b492-30e222c5f5a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8bec425d-e327-5350-ad55-2bf5d8347c9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-100757" :question/problem "${(6\\cdot 8)}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n{(6\\cdot 8)}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5}={6}^{3}\\cdot {8}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule for exponents, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3}\\cdot {8}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5} & ={6}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5}\\cdot {8}^{3} \\\\  & ={6}^{3+5}\\cdot {8}^{3} \\\\  & ={6}^{8}\\cdot {8}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d32c85e-20d0-5fd2-a609-287a06932ccb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502179 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d4c53a1-b239-5b29-8440-f632e3a00d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{4}\\cdot {3}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501546} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa97fff-f307-512a-a0df-4801533ea472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot {3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501671} {:answer/id #uuid "84236fbb-6291-5f93-81e2-cd38a8816289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501719} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a0ea31-2173-5c5d-9e00-40527e7cf292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{12}\\cdot {3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501862} {:answer/id #uuid "fb321c56-57a3-55ec-b817-8c07ac7ecf7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501719} :answer-field/id #uuid "88004d56-effe-5e83-a749-a2b7c08b3fa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ef8e162-2bcb-5b53-83cc-147de68407b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-68952" :question/problem "${2}^{3}\\cdot {(3\\cdot 2)}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n{2}^{3}\\cdot {(3\\cdot 2)}^{4}={2}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule for exponents, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{4} & ={2}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{4}\\cdot {3}^{4} \\\\  & ={2}^{3+4}\\cdot {3}^{4} \\\\  & ={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "49308f5f-8de3-5dbe-bf1a-8b336864e19e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502168}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502198 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a73b112-23d9-5ce7-b7f6-9cc9b1ea6ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501541} {:answer/id #uuid "5173cabb-209f-59b0-bb30-f3195ff21e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501614} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8cacfb-384f-5813-911b-4115a5b7ba6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501667} {:answer/id #uuid "8d016a81-6b30-515f-b97d-3efba5f2f640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{7}\\cdot 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501737} {:answer/id #uuid "f91702ba-eae7-53c8-b6a7-9bd48502b51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501667} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf72de5c-8edd-59cf-9c2b-dc6c69c9659d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22282018-2ca4-523a-8e4c-90a2c94b5770" :question/math-academy-id "q-68493" :question/problem "${(\\frac{2}{3})}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9}=\\frac{{2}^{7}}{{3}^{7}}\\cdot {3}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{2}^{7}}{{3}^{7}}\\cdot {3}^{9}={2}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{9}}{{3}^{7}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{2}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{9}}{{3}^{7}}={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9-7}={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{2}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ad914d59-631f-5b15-8d69-7505a90e3736" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502179}}] :activity/title "Combining the Rules of Exponents" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501456}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060770} :activity/id #uuid "9b6ebd1c-f083-5c13-ba2f-eefcd05878c6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162 :topic/id #uuid "df4f6208-5d6c-5dc9-bd38-c7d85e32fb73" :topic/math-academy-id 239} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060969 :tutorial/content "Consider the region below, which represents the inequality $2 < x < 5.$\n\n![](images/fe/feeae0bfa9b29e6f034a3fd33d1e9fbd369840835af2d6588892ee6edc2fa120.png)\n\nWe can represent this inequality using **interval notation** as follows:\n\n$$\n(2,5)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\phantom{0})$ tell us that the endpoints $x=2$ and $x=5$ are *not* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval does not include the endpoints, we say that it is **open**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (2,5).\n$$\n\nThe symbol $\\in$ means \"lies in\" or \"belongs to.\" So, the above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (open) interval $(2,5).$*\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "dbd2ff75-3253-5df6-ac32-55d5e3a95cf5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6750 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "53a0851d-e1e9-5f13-a1f9-1e0ad720c677" :step/math-academy-id 40878 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059385 :question/id #uuid "9096883e-6dd0-5dd1-8e9c-176775edadba" :question/math-academy-id "e-10785"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f45f69-d8e7-5e79-a1ec-38d8912cbc19" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502752} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502749}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Open Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "6325c0ca-8510-5c9d-8b87-b9042fe377ed" :step/math-academy-id 40879 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060970 :tutorial/content "Some intervals may include one parenthesis and one bracket. \n\nFor example, consider the following interval:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{\\big[}}} 1,4 {\\color{red}{\\big)}}\n$$\n\nTo express this interval as an inequality, we note the following:\n\n- The left bracket $\\,\\color{blue}[\\,$ tells us that left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}$ for this endpoint.\n\n- The right parenthesis $\\,\\color{red})\\,$ tells us that the right endpoint $4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{<      }}\\,$ for this endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq  }}\\,\\,   x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, 4.\n$$\n\nThe interval can be graphed as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a67872e8193c2bcc249a9a9006acb916909099e4babdbe5cdfa6d2643c10ad2.png)\n\nIntervals with one bracket and one parenthesis are called **half-open** (or **half-closed**)." :tutorial/id #uuid "633202af-a5bd-58ac-a806-89458305a7a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6751 :tutorial/title "Half-Open Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "149280d9-7b92-52c0-ab69-a011aca574b3" :step/math-academy-id 40882 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059386 :question/id #uuid "b828f144-b8b8-5eda-94f1-43cccdcb1aaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10786"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdf77469-6c2a-5339-b216-3bc5e2316e4d" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502628} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542222}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "9d9e1551-d8ec-536f-9036-9555d43eae22" :step/math-academy-id 40883 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060774}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059387 :question/id #uuid "45d05c7d-e278-552f-a524-926d4a57d870" :question/math-academy-id "e-10787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8c3e092-d9ff-5dc9-ad1e-f31aa2b226ba" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502851} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502843}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Number Lines"} :step/id #uuid "6f9b14a3-07a8-5f6b-9c69-4df4a979ee4a" :step/math-academy-id 40887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/id #uuid "6d4bd0a2-0961-54c1-b781-b470d420d848" :question/math-academy-id "e-10816"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f519fa9-de89-5b00-aa02-312cf8e32cba" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502720} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553554}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Closed Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "082973dc-66d1-5d9c-9dc3-ae132f451431" :step/math-academy-id 40993 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060973 :tutorial/content "Now consider the region below, which represents the inequality $-3 \\leq x \\leq 1.$\n\n![](images/38/38c5bf5408bbbeb0f63e198e0564a37e8fe6a34d7f2ce340751d8000443123ed.png)\n\nWe express this inequality using interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe brackets $[\\phantom{0}]$ tell us that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=1$ *are* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval includes the endpoints, we say that it is **closed**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1].\n$$\n\nThe above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (closed) interval $[-3,1].$*\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "27f305da-f09b-53e5-a8b8-a30d29eb0f15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6821 :tutorial/title "Closed Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "b373d760-0d5d-5ec6-a830-9c562d00e5be" :step/math-academy-id 41408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060771}}] :activity/title "Interval Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059917}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111678} :activity/id #uuid "f14be6fb-7490-5e50-9e2d-7ccd72daa91c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310 :topic/id #uuid "eb6655db-68e9-5ef3-bf7a-81223e92dac0" :topic/math-academy-id 1414} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111814 :tutorial/content "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.** \n\nFor example, consider the following equation: \n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ x+5-x & =x+5-x \\\\ 5 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x.$ This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3,$ into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ 3+5 & =3+5 \\\\ 8 & =8.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values \n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." :tutorial/id #uuid "edb6ae26-e191-5d7d-9ce8-ad9ee36655f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11389 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "eab36dd4-7684-5fb5-a213-f02f98e0da39" :step/math-academy-id 476188 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110372 :question/id #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18854"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476103} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492056}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "310129a9-81a2-54d5-b227-633c2cb1b8d6" :step/math-academy-id 476189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111818 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x=2x,\\,\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{1}{3}x\\,-2.16x=-2.16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =bx \\\\ ax & =(a)x \\\\ ax & =ax\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =ax \\\\ ax-ax & =ax-ax \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0,$ is always true, regardless of the value of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "8428afc7-2e9a-55d0-9f13-7a2acdf8fd7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13429 :tutorial/title "Conditions for an Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "888ce267-7f48-5074-a1ab-42caf954a38c" :step/math-academy-id 476192 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111815 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2qx = 10x,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b.$ Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2q = 10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =10 \\\\ \\frac{2q}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5.$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2qx & =10x \\\\ 2(5)x & =10x \\\\ 10x & =10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =10x \\\\ 10x-10x & =10x-10x \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "0d28dc5e-68de-53d0-a57c-9072a89eccbb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11486 :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "2f94c8cb-7345-56bc-b884-f0ac3f242910" :step/math-academy-id 476193 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110368 :question/id #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17212"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476197} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553489}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} :step/id #uuid "1e04eac3-4a93-5d72-9a90-2b1099628885" :step/math-academy-id 476194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111819 :tutorial/content "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =cx+d \\\\ ax+b & =(a)x+(b) \\\\ ax+b & =ax+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =ax+b \\\\ ax+b-ax & =ax+b-ax \\\\ b & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b,$ is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "7498a4f8-36b5-5d1d-b953-893581bfa9aa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13430 :tutorial/title "Conditions for a General Linear Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "1d109dfd-fdee-5718-a3d2-a9a96f41ffd0" :step/math-academy-id 476197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111817 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "dc5c5f3e-0731-5ab3-8afd-a2cf24228db1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13403 :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters in General Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "01172b4e-9bb6-512f-9e14-1b2ad76e2a6f" :step/math-academy-id 476198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110371 :question/id #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" :question/math-academy-id "e-18852"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477026} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492013}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "c8fdb7d5-63c2-55ac-a293-d9536babfe47" :step/math-academy-id 476199}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110810}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549131} :activity/id #uuid "53e407aa-b874-5ab8-ad52-b6329fa3b408" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310 :topic/id #uuid "eb6655db-68e9-5ef3-bf7a-81223e92dac0" :topic/math-academy-id 1414} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe4c39bf-4560-5825-a22a-76a5df230cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621} :answer-field/id #uuid "58355121-2d5d-5aa8-91c2-79f41befa005" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1e37e75-7d0c-5923-8a40-5f706dc21da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288584" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+3=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=2.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=2$ and $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=2+3=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3b9efce2-cc34-5966-9ee7-777169e666e6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eab2015-3700-56bf-ac92-fd0a94f661e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548387} {:answer/id #uuid "598ab84f-b8ec-5739-8cb0-fcbd36abfb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548519} {:answer/id #uuid "96f6e7b6-efcd-5a03-89c8-76e8676edae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548648} {:answer/id #uuid "987b69a0-afb9-52c1-b363-d34b352bfb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548656} {:answer/id #uuid "f71c8c9c-cd96-5e6e-b046-0e4cdbb2a210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548656} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0b4382-89cc-5b45-b568-d6f0542bad8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6ec3c46-2316-5f98-be70-c232b5719be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288560" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(r-7)x=-x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=r-7,\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nr-7=-1.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "70e3dbbc-f265-58ec-abc4-fa61aa670f9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc56c0e-abb3-5e53-818e-6aa51cc4af57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548439} {:answer/id #uuid "36c25fee-f58b-5409-a5a6-477628433eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548463} {:answer/id #uuid "5ade53e2-0afe-522f-a54b-d5db209e2043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548522} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3dbfdf-aa6d-5049-8e76-3fb7df01f4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548560} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1a1e15-d44d-54e8-a331-6ddc82706537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548595} :answer-field/id #uuid "e27fc349-c24d-5039-a135-0c2dd2d434ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "61992611-6e48-5a2e-9a4c-b280b994c51d" :question/math-academy-id "q-288561" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(t+4)x=2x\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=t+4,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nt+4=2.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9c38efa4-70b9-572d-aabd-a33044024fc7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549131 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a449b2-0875-581b-a2ec-8813a227c1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6f283f-3592-5248-baf7-ca782b7d8c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295530} {:answer/id #uuid "4009ef03-e229-5324-9dbd-214305b60cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295633} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfca85a-c698-5e36-ba67-02a55f783f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295715} {:answer/id #uuid "d199324f-9733-59f2-82cf-8e4f45ae01ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} :answer-field/id #uuid "1739b830-13d4-586f-9761-4372557e83e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "435ae769-5577-5924-a2b3-9f23f7e0ccc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-287971" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(p-3)x=4x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=p-3,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\np-3=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=7.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a60530a2-2052-544d-a2a2-f6b827880aca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549108}}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548342}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069912} :activity/id #uuid "f525c0ff-8172-5646-b7ba-2dd13dd2cdf5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049394 :topic/id #uuid "f121c6bf-38c7-56a2-930a-199717f2bc16" :topic/math-academy-id 398} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070012 :tutorial/content "The **$y$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $y$-axis. For instance, consider the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and has a slope of $3.$ How can we find the $y$-intercept of this line? \n\n![](images/c3/c307a60266b77cb497a5c5b02a6d9518a3b31e22b1ec97afb1d2b40f488982cb.png)\n\nWe already know values for $x$, $y$ and $m,$ so we can just substitute them into the equation $y=mx+b$ and then solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =mx+b \\\\ (-1) & =(3)(1)+b \\\\ -1 & =3+b \\\\ b & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is ${\\color{blue}b}=-4.$ The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-4).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f9c0e73-8a80-5472-bf82-4c78c873c181" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8059 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "32960d35-0132-5c05-a0dc-e73600e6a188" :step/math-academy-id 67150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069913}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068460 :question/id #uuid "4ad21d3a-bf40-5b65-8ba6-320d853e6cff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc85ca17-8a08-5473-9473-dce954132bd5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049716}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492010}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Line Given a Point on the Line and Its Slope"} :step/id #uuid "05d03a89-9aa4-5b0f-97cd-69809a74c97d" :step/math-academy-id 67151 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068461 :question/id #uuid "fdc3a6e9-080b-5e2a-b5a4-3af01c7018ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12870"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e985ae8-05ba-590a-b504-4c84884679f4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049061}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553562}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points"} :step/id #uuid "fab7c6fb-b6fe-5b55-adee-114f45b728d4" :step/math-academy-id 67155 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070013 :tutorial/content "The **$x$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $x$-axis. For instance, consider the line $y = 2x - 4$. How can we find the $x$-intercept of this line?\n\n![](images/57/57a31eab932c948358e7ab6e428707a6a4b9f551bc956554c3a16b659b5f7988.png)\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-4 \\\\ (0) & =2x-4 \\\\ 4 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$**-intercept** is $x=2.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(2,0).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fbd0b44d-8460-5058-9b94-c21852d09a4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8060 :tutorial/title "Finding the X-Intercept of a Straight Line"} :step/id #uuid "27dfa118-efd1-5317-966f-90a083509c55" :step/math-academy-id 67159 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069916}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068462 :question/id #uuid "ec5bebf6-58d8-5b4d-900c-3c024628647c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12871"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63461668-4f76-52a4-8115-1ab135eef9e3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050532}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551251}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Intercept of a Line Given a Point and a Slope"} :step/id #uuid "ebe74c08-cc12-564f-b8bf-ce093355d7a9" :step/math-academy-id 67160 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068463 :question/id #uuid "409dfb1f-7400-5539-9dea-6c2d1b6b7ef0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12872"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f66989ce-aac5-5737-b60b-6ed703634cd5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492003}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intercept of a Horizontal or Vertical Line"} :step/id #uuid "8c918cb7-6f6b-5686-99e8-54b94b6009e4" :step/math-academy-id 67164}] :activity/title "Properties of Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068943}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503780} :activity/id #uuid "9b65c706-a56f-5043-b155-869786ec18fd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049394 :topic/id #uuid "f121c6bf-38c7-56a2-930a-199717f2bc16" :topic/math-academy-id 398} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60e87123-16db-5a92-8cb2-8a23c0aeaba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503170} {:answer/id #uuid "698c2d3c-6a55-59a5-8c8b-9b9708fddb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503187} {:answer/id #uuid "97d26e7c-a075-5ed5-a723-528daa017501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503282} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc76194-9c0b-5ed2-8b4e-4b1e27f0b5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503361} {:answer/id #uuid "f9554e82-405c-52e6-af3f-b2b480146aaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503170} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1a378b-e807-52c5-ac81-76d5297f2e2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4eef31d-a7fd-5ae0-810a-624f060a825b" :question/math-academy-id "q-6902" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the line that passes through the points $(2,7)$ and $(3,11).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-7}{3-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=4$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=4x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+b \\\\ 7 & =4(2)+b \\\\ 7 & =8+b \\\\ 7-8 & =b \\\\ -1 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the equation of the line is $y=4x-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "45872dd6-e531-5118-a656-32545845e64a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503816 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "196a504d-8519-53de-a140-45e7f8cea19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503033} {:answer/id #uuid "4098c8f0-90f5-57e1-ba2c-2a4d7e248771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503104} {:answer/id #uuid "50a00234-aaec-5fc9-a334-a039e8d088d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503135} {:answer/id #uuid "77e09b7f-fef5-597d-84f8-1e7e8d6c7194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503223} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ac371a-7bc0-5123-8664-79c20ac774c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503104} :answer-field/id #uuid "f749aa0a-56e8-5fac-8b2c-39890015d4a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4459c635-09f2-5d35-8ba8-5eb9bfaa5126" :question/math-academy-id "q-44304" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line $y=-2x+10.$" :question/worked-solution "At the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+10 \\\\ 0 & =-2x+10 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=5.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(5,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "b3f3f538-e0c9-5b52-9abc-3ac0e6f13003"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33213927-0872-5481-b492-8c2a15d7bc5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=10x-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503073} {:answer/id #uuid "3e07aaa1-5380-511b-82d1-705ddf05637f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=10x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503096} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fd756d-7353-5103-b953-d571e22e7381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=10x+47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503312} {:answer/id #uuid "ca218148-c955-580f-8eb5-5479cf23735d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=10x+17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503386} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7e9268-f0e3-598c-acbf-cc02c162d4d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=10x-47" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503073} :answer-field/id #uuid "806cc9db-3354-5070-98b7-05a7234f89ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9be101d3-9545-55c8-953e-3e73d5bfedaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-6944" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the line that passes through the points $(4,7)$ and $(5,17).$" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17-7}{5-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=10$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=10x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10x+b \\\\ 7 & =10(4)+b \\\\ 7 & =40+b \\\\ 7-40 & =b \\\\ -33 & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the line is $y=10x-33.$"} :step/id #uuid "fb3f8a8a-5717-5f57-b96f-677651c9d6bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503816}}] :activity/title "Properties of Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502970}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097496} :activity/id #uuid "23266c50-a061-5ecc-b648-05f71a1b5ec6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619 :topic/id #uuid "0536fa23-cd17-50b6-b180-7b50962fb1ee" :topic/math-academy-id 1153} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097924 :tutorial/content "An **exponential function** is a function where a variable is contained within an exponent. For example, the function below is an exponential function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3^x\n$$\n\nJust like any other function, we can evaluate it for some particular value of $x,$ say at $x={\\color{blue}{4}},$ by substituting this value of $x$ into the function:\n\n$$\nf(4) = 3^{\\color{blue}{4}} = 81\n$$\n\nAnother example of an exponential function is $g(x)$ below:\n\n$$\ng(x) = 16\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function at $x={\\color{blue}{4}}$ using the power of quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(4) & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{4}}{{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExponential functions are of huge practical importance in science, engineering, economics, and many other disciplines. Let's practice evaluating them." :tutorial/id #uuid "155b9a63-2c55-5f26-a41f-b777e7afa911" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8843 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "28a95194-58c2-5aab-b5ec-1edc7f9c4953" :step/math-academy-id 166109 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097497}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096390 :question/id #uuid "d03736b3-a7d8-52b3-894d-4ce2d04ee31c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9283940a-cad8-51b5-b8c2-dc42ad8de264" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048683}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488193}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Exponential Function"} :step/id #uuid "e368b5da-f79a-5928-9c93-43f4a0dee7a5" :step/math-academy-id 166110 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097498}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096391 :question/id #uuid "a83c1301-18cf-5622-8a4a-73f9d18e36b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14260"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bab04f2-5e9a-55e7-9669-63e15938be0a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553580}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function in a Modeling Problem"} :step/id #uuid "103957a3-310c-54ce-8227-8f216b6f4683" :step/math-academy-id 166114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097499}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097925 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given the following exponential function:\n\n$$\nf(t) = C\\cdot 4^t\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that $f(3) = 128.$ Can we use this information to find the unknown constant $C?$\n\nTo solve this problem, we substitute $t=3$ and $f(t) = 128$ and solve for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}128 & =C\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 128 & =C\\cdot 64 \\\\ \\frac{128}{64} & =C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C=2.$ So our function expressed in full is\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2\\cdot 4^t.\n$$\n\nWe sometimes say that the **initial value** of the function is $2,$ because if we evaluate $f$ at $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(0) = 2\\cdot 4^0 = 2\\cdot 1 = 2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6820cdab-7e0a-5a90-adb1-880e4eb4a8de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8846 :tutorial/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant"} :step/id #uuid "0d53b42c-c931-5e58-b073-7217b0bb9ac4" :step/math-academy-id 166118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096659 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096392 :question/id #uuid "ca8cd4d6-8136-59bb-a033-6ba9ae8c06ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-14262"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cee2b0-6cc8-5281-8ea2-02400b2980e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547021}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Initial Value"} :step/id #uuid "546da0c9-4c8f-57c2-9e29-200dc3ee9f2d" :step/math-academy-id 166119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097926 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following exponential function:\n\n$$\nh(x) = 2^x\n$$\n\nCan we find the value of $x$ that gives $h(x)=64?$ Indeed, we can. To do this, we'll need to use our knowledge of exponential equations. \n\nFirst, we use the fact that $h(x)=64$ to write our equation as\n\n$$\n2^x = 64.\n$$\n\nThis is an exponential equation. Since we know that $64$ is a power of $2,$ we can solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x} & =64 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{6} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "94964a23-690b-5cea-8e8c-5946840cca5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8848 :tutorial/title "Solving for an Unknown Input Value"} :step/id #uuid "2934358a-1be2-5bac-84d5-feb8432e9804" :step/math-academy-id 166122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096660 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096393 :question/id #uuid "02cbb721-a880-5600-8093-fc768ae5e993" :question/math-academy-id "e-14264"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a21f047-5100-5f8b-9448-a5c2044e4e65" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488280}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Input Value"} :step/id #uuid "499e92bc-8919-5fbf-8915-9df0c0cca3d7" :step/math-academy-id 166123}] :activity/title "Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096918}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509046} :activity/id #uuid "7c388d04-2b80-59a8-b4dd-4cabcf69a693" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619 :topic/id #uuid "0536fa23-cd17-50b6-b180-7b50962fb1ee" :topic/math-academy-id 1153} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363f8683-7ce6-519e-b516-88ec0639bc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\,293$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508471} {:answer/id #uuid "7999765f-0877-5da0-8674-68c82949589f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$11\\,185$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508568} {:answer/id #uuid "83349184-0745-5f0a-adb8-dc217a25b73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3\\,645$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508584} {:answer/id #uuid "bc607cfb-7458-58ee-9fe1-447d861318c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$32\\,805$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508676} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f48a1a-dec7-5522-a1f4-4f63c0ce08a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$234\\,375$ subscribers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508471} :answer-field/id #uuid "595c2d53-60ff-5406-b569-8b0372ade376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1571510a-8d3e-5007-9b28-e17c3cb5bde3" :question/math-academy-id "q-112148" :question/problem "The number of subscribers to a video channel is given by $S(t)=5\\cdot (2.4)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in days since the channel was opened. How many subscribers will the channel have after a week? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "To get the number of subscribers to the video channel after a week ($7$ days), we evaluate the function at $t=7.$ Substituting $t=7$ into the function $S(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(7) & =5\\cdot (2.4)^{7} \\\\  & ≈2\\,293.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after a week, the video channel will have $2\\,293$ subscribers."} :step/id #uuid "2c28505c-84c4-5461-afbd-0a3de162478c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9063cde2-3330-509e-ae49-cc65532f4b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508607}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508607} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b57ab6-1b1d-515d-affc-7350d9f0ce89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4fda74bd-4783-5b76-9154-8ddd3936c772" :question/math-academy-id "q-218594" :question/problem "If $f(x)=A\\cdot {2}^{x}$ and $f(6)=192,$ then $A=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=6$ and $f(x)=192$ into the expression for $f(x)$ and then solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}192 & =A\\cdot {2}^{6} \\\\ \\frac{192}{{2}^{6}} & =A \\\\ \\frac{192}{64} & =A \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "d1d64943-9a98-5436-a9cf-ea3fcf7adb24"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7d7512-00dd-53a1-a339-e816ce6f9bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0.04$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508412} {:answer/id #uuid "4a011cf2-f11f-556b-9630-c5475d45f2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0.2$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508497} {:answer/id #uuid "9528fa7d-db24-588f-95a3-5f14223601ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508611} {:answer/id #uuid "a758b6c6-f6c3-5428-a865-833457877121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0.02$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508640} {:answer/id #uuid "d808d943-003d-59e8-a1b2-bb5ce6ae207f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$0.4$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508412} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bbf46a9-a271-5e94-8244-41b134f4a887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4c0de90-e894-5c05-811c-ac221194e0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112129" :question/problem "A five gallons water bottle is leaking its content. The number of gallons of water left inside the bottle after $t$ minutes is given by the formula $W(t)=5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}.$ How many gallons of water will be inside the bottle after $3$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "To get the number of gallons of water inside the bottle after $3$ minutes, we evaluate the function at $t=3.$ Substituting $t=3$ into the function $W(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{125} \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after $3$ minutes, there will be $0.04$ gallons of water inside the bottle."} :step/id #uuid "f31a4f50-f5a8-571a-bc6d-1143b83e36a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509006}}] :activity/title "Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508381}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547106} :activity/id #uuid "fbcd23bd-0b5e-522e-a224-1cd518e64d90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619 :topic/id #uuid "0536fa23-cd17-50b6-b180-7b50962fb1ee" :topic/math-academy-id 1153} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc0268ec-d8fa-5068-89a2-d047adff26bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "162" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546737}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546737} :answer-field/id #uuid "a498868f-8302-53e1-96f6-a5c4ab372013" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7a913f31-a39a-5842-bec8-7be0ed73cc2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-218599" :question/problem "If $f(x)=A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and $f(4)=2,$ then $A=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=4$ and $f(x)=2$ into the expression for $f(x)$ and solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4} \\\\ 2 & =A\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{4}}{{3}^{4}}) \\\\ 2 & =A\\cdot \\frac{1}{81} \\\\ 2\\cdot 81 & =A \\\\ 162 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=162.$"} :step/id #uuid "24c4d8ea-2c2d-5bf1-8012-7f901ed738b4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb188b7-1bba-52cb-abb3-fd6aed751092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546280} {:answer/id #uuid "2544cee4-9901-5260-a310-d276e35ad5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546333} {:answer/id #uuid "3735ad7d-4556-5e39-a71d-f32c39e15424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546370} {:answer/id #uuid "8720c01d-8df0-5742-be97-36d70ae63773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546550} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0d70e3-37d6-5e7a-96ef-4b4a41e04706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546370} :answer-field/id #uuid "84915d34-7d23-593e-8953-0481a5d952d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d902ef6-57b9-5fc3-a3a5-f189fbbd0124" :question/math-academy-id "q-61724" :question/problem "If $g(x)=M\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ and $g(4)=3,$ then $M=$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=4$ and $g(x)=3$ into the expression for $g(x)$ and solve for $M,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =M\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\ 3 & =M\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{4}}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\ 3 & =M\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 3\\cdot 16 & =M \\\\ 48 & =M\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $M=48.$"} :step/id #uuid "400b2935-82a5-5288-b8ed-ee8bd5f66ac6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547144}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f86ea7-b245-5854-99ca-445e07a33913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546361} {:answer/id #uuid "345e8697-48b9-551d-896e-1cee7485be04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "248" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546364} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb74a52-e9ea-5852-b670-f8fc81bc2b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546424} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff1664d-bf13-5f15-9c2d-ee07c74dcf74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546607} {:answer/id #uuid "c656e398-ebf6-5ea7-85fa-6a3e66c74967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546692}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546361} :answer-field/id #uuid "a47053c5-18d3-5ce9-95e6-882a410af16b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c55619be-bc04-5da4-9e98-241a09beba3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-112141" :question/problem "The growth of a mice colony is given by the formula $M(t)=6\\cdot {2}^{t},$ where $t$ is the time, in months, since the beginning of the observation. How many mice will the colony have after $5$ months?" :question/worked-solution "To get the number of mice in the colony after $5$ months, we evaluate the function at $t=5.$ Substituting $t=5$ into the function $M(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(5) & =6\\cdot {2}^{5} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =192.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after $5$ months there will be $192$ mice in the colony."} :step/id #uuid "b7c0f0f3-0bc3-52e8-9529-1d0edd9dbc37" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547099}}] :activity/title "Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546253}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074629} :activity/id #uuid "a0efe329-d527-57d7-9005-092c45b3c758" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050273 :topic/id #uuid "3bc5deb9-7ddf-5f6d-86b1-c0f92239ac2d" :topic/math-academy-id 558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073635 :question/id #uuid "a86fdc21-1c98-5929-8282-aed055b1b053" :question/math-academy-id "e-4745"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a94905c-48fe-5662-bb6c-2153f7725648" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050195}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491170}] :knowledge-point/title "Problem Solving Using the Ratio of Two Integers"} :step/id #uuid "18311dd6-9a0a-534e-9a8d-3794869d485b" :step/math-academy-id 17329 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073725 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073636 :question/id #uuid "52c7bc18-eedc-504f-bbae-a501944834a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4746"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea092786-a281-534e-803b-90e6c23b90a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049357}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491214}] :knowledge-point/title "Problem Solving Using the Ratio of a Fraction and an Integer"} :step/id #uuid "b3df2fe0-94ac-5bcc-a3b1-52b4d1b4236b" :step/math-academy-id 17330 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073726 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073637 :question/id #uuid "4f61ec9c-9b87-5b25-9543-44b6043b7b4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4747"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d34e60c9-710b-50ca-a491-adc2b50e6edc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049616}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553520}] :knowledge-point/title "Problem Solving Using the Ratio of Two Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "347b63ef-9c0b-5688-8e27-d7df776461a0" :step/math-academy-id 17331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075005 :tutorial/content "Unit rates can be used to solve many real-world problems in which we are given two quantities and wish to find what happens to one quantity when we vary the other.\n\nFor example, we might know the total cost of a specific number of items and be asked to find the cost for a different number of the same item.\n\nTo solve a problem using unit rates, the first step is to compute the unit rate by dividing the two given quantities. Then, we can use the unit rate to calculate the desired quantity.\n\nLet's see some examples!" :tutorial/id #uuid "fc623ecb-782c-5182-90e7-e8b6720b2780" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3313 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7987d98c-69c0-57a6-b100-cd3d9e53820b" :step/math-academy-id 18413 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074616}}] :activity/title "Solving Problems Using Unit Rates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073976}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111719} :activity/id #uuid "01608397-be37-5684-a088-261c3d612344" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050476 :topic/id #uuid "4cb923ba-b9fd-54eb-8613-ff3a3ef21278" :topic/math-academy-id 1463} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111857 :tutorial/content "Let's find the midpoint $M$ of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ on a number line, if $A$ corresponds to $x_1= 3$ and $B$ corresponds to $x_2=7,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/11/115797e11eb3e434d5faefe412a8cefea216b6190436d408d199ac31d0da7210.png)\n\n \n\nThe midpoint of a line segment splits it into two segments of equal length.\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to $M,$ we use the following **midpoint formula**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x_1=3$ and $x_2=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{3+7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=5.$\n\n![](images/fc/fc0a47734756e12ec117db92d8f0273ae564c03ead88ce86eac82005be6feaf4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7b14d3c0-0c84-55bc-be21-3359fbc5f6ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2347 :tutorial/title "The Midpoint Formula"} :step/id #uuid "f54758a4-a9bd-55d8-8fe5-726424159290" :step/math-academy-id 10013 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111835 :tutorial/content "The **midpoint** of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length. For example, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede582389ad936b02a6074a3fb6543e730ae955baf9784147f133f9492a35708.png)\n\nThe midpoint splits the line segment into two congruent segments,\n\n$$\n\\overline{PM}\\cong \\overline{QM}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice as long as the segments $\\overline{PM}$ and $\\overline{QM}$:\n\n$$\nPQ = 2PM, \\qquad PQ = 2QM\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9e476e67-9dd4-5098-be96-fc805e9e97f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2165 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fb8d8caf-a880-55f9-8ec4-027667c4d158" :step/math-academy-id 8710 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110377 :question/id #uuid "f46e0c53-c110-5990-b3ea-5dd27991cdc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2557"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f6f3cc2-e0b5-5263-896d-53369438a2f8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503691}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Midpoint Using the Midpoint Formula"} :step/id #uuid "071ff184-b63f-5999-8a03-a464cb40e4a4" :step/math-academy-id 8711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110378 :question/id #uuid "7c337d65-54d7-5264-b750-1a7f7bcd07f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-2558"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb88880e-8b94-5223-b3fb-a4d80e795a00" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492904}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Midpoints to Calculate an Unknown Value From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "b8ed3410-17c6-5b4e-801f-204a8559cabe" :step/math-academy-id 8712 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110379 :question/id #uuid "6e321a78-087f-5962-a88c-e61bdc10de9f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2559"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4a4cad5-d0b0-51cb-921f-31a96e8e5eda" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553555}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Properties of Midpoints to Calculate an Unknown Value From a Description"} :step/id #uuid "143b8bf4-dc04-56fe-9f49-a538e4b75644" :step/math-academy-id 8713 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110380 :question/id #uuid "c79d8054-b4f5-57e3-b816-5ac07ccfa90d" :question/math-academy-id "e-2560"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c451d750-c40f-5b3f-9cd2-9eec197809de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048760}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492865}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Midpoint Formula to Calculate an Endpoint"} :step/id #uuid "45c8bbe7-572c-5f45-8330-89214603ae4d" :step/math-academy-id 8714}] :activity/title "Midpoints" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110835}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079450} :activity/id #uuid "fa63b04f-e0ce-592f-b214-495b5c1b67a9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050566 :topic/id #uuid "546b628e-ba40-5207-8646-07c283c27604" :topic/math-academy-id 631} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079616 :tutorial/content "A **function** is a rule where *every* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value. \n\nAs an example, let's consider the rule defined by\n\n$$\nf(x) = x+3\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ can be any number from the **set** $\\{1,2,3,4\\}.$ A set is a collection of numbers we can use in our rule. \n\nLet's calculate the possible values of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =1+3=4 \\\\ f(2) & =2+3=5 \\\\ f(3) & =3+3=6 \\\\ f(4) & =4+3=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this rule using a **mapping diagram**, like the one shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4714001592222932934cb7edd5852542a2e990e6680daad85e2db88b9c7cef47.png)\n\nThis rule *is* a function because *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output." :tutorial/id #uuid "88ebc343-41ab-500a-8668-451bbf36ec8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "93acbf1d-7504-5269-982c-7785e36bfa3d" :step/math-academy-id 3581 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078527 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078142 :question/id #uuid "6d57991d-4cdb-546b-80c5-4df14f1b385d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1096"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cc2167e-d318-54d5-8b67-47afbc65f530" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553493}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Mapping Diagram Is a Function"} :step/id #uuid "953349df-46b8-543b-a772-87c3e0ffd977" :step/math-academy-id 3585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079454}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079694 :tutorial/content "Now let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/b0/b02a511f8043bb7e39df2de082befa267f795948ea6c1cc1ef38eb9df4a8cb1d.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is assigned to *more than one* output. In this case, the input $x=1$ is mapped to *both* $2$ and $-2.$\n\nFinally, let's consider the rule given by the following mapping diagram:\n\n![](images/be/bea4ee8835a6111e105670863edede9450ad26dbfbea631454d5c8d6cd76a6a1.png)\n\nThis rule is *not* a function because *at least one* input is not assigned to an output. In this case, the input $0$ is not assigned an output." :tutorial/id #uuid "f58c4e80-86e3-5b65-99a3-2a16208231e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5913 :tutorial/title "Examples of Rules That Are Not Functions"} :step/id #uuid "4034d183-7b91-5f03-a1ca-b71ce2a3ea8c" :step/math-academy-id 36150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078528 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078381 :question/id #uuid "279a2ac1-2577-5ec3-8eb4-8496650c6b54" :question/math-academy-id "e-9905"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b869d240-38f8-52c8-bb87-110d7ac616b8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510456}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Table Is a Function"} :step/id #uuid "d65897aa-199d-5bb6-a05a-f5877fe63002" :step/math-academy-id 37242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079695 :tutorial/content "When a rule is represented as a table, we have a function if and only if all values in the *first row* are *distinct* (i.e., different).\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\nThe rule defined by the following table is a function. Notice that all values in the first row are distinct. Every input value is assigned to exactly one output value.\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, the rule defined by the following table is *not* a function. Notice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}3$ twice. The input value $3$ is assigned to two output values ($0$ and $-1$).\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $2$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $4$ | $\\color{red}3$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $7$ | $-1$ | $5$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5d2e3d4-e985-565a-81f8-c8b8e93dc73d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6156 :tutorial/title "Rules Represented Using Tables"} :step/id #uuid "53c16c15-06ad-566b-bb58-7baf803dbf46" :step/math-academy-id 37341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079696 :tutorial/content "So, we must remember that a rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f92a951f-7b57-51b4-b130-bf0d4c0da4a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6161 :tutorial/title "A Summary"} :step/id #uuid "e2bd4f1a-fe61-532e-a324-4c924a280b8e" :step/math-academy-id 37364 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079451}}] :activity/title "Visual Representations of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078655}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510497} :activity/id #uuid "02347787-e68b-52a4-afbc-1ad4be765c4f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050566 :topic/id #uuid "546b628e-ba40-5207-8646-07c283c27604" :topic/math-academy-id 631} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b452610-1a90-593f-89a8-1c4334f8692e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since both sets have the same number of elements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509841} {:answer/id #uuid "a9162203-d658-5d42-9df6-3ec42dbfef2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since each output is related to at least one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510049} {:answer/id #uuid "c10dd595-c5fb-5d9b-b6ec-8500d9d83c3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510092} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc3a002-b6d3-5418-8801-26229653dddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510122} {:answer/id #uuid "e849c16f-4c33-5f1d-9015-49309af959d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No, since both sets have the same number of elements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510092} :answer-field/id #uuid "0509729a-0cf8-5abe-9a21-a913a1bfda28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f8427f7-1892-52e0-9e03-33a7a2709f34" :question/math-academy-id "q-6728" :question/problem "![](images/99/998d9b7d5d9e342f58add32f80cddfcb6f01e255497f87376c0e39e01abb67ba.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value."} :step/id #uuid "40e19e3b-3dd3-5aaf-ade8-710dfe052f40" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510513}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cad4dd8-ca6d-55ab-9dc5-081de80e69a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077655} {:answer/id #uuid "001094b5-522b-5aff-87d8-cdfd551294ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No, since there is at least one output with more than one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077656} {:answer/id #uuid "3024a1e1-3c8f-5f6d-ab79-bde1c13d5709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since each input is assigned to exactly one output" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077657} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd5fdb7-c2a9-539d-b5b1-60da0e4d051a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Yes, since each output is related to at least one input" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077658} {:answer/id #uuid "6008c3f2-d2aa-5fd1-96b6-5ae4ce87bd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No, since both sets have different numbers of elements" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186077657} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d9b076-e24e-5725-bd8f-2293f79ac91a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "77dee980-a89b-544a-9fab-994ab35aaa5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-60621" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aaba52792efd52cae40bed740b50f3f92f54e0f13436c16ffc8358ff8200cd76.png)\n\nIs the above rule a function?" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n \n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\n \n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value."} :step/id #uuid "44c9b8f5-0dec-5758-9859-bd384b4e4b61"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510513 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2327a800-cbca-5988-8f96-dcae5bdc00c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-6,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509762} {:answer/id #uuid "2b22e163-0fc6-5bd7-bfc7-4b03d09bad8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509777} {:answer/id #uuid "3551050d-f232-5fde-b085-deb54a7b7231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509803} {:answer/id #uuid "74addcf0-8125-5f4d-a238-5f72f1ddecb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509935} {:answer/id #uuid "b145389b-1dbe-5fde-882c-d9f9182c3df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(9,26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509777} :answer-field/id #uuid "61917ff0-105b-5bca-9ac2-b0132beab63d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d26e72e3-99fa-5024-9c2a-cf9b61a0b079" :question/math-academy-id "q-31655" :question/problem "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | ? | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $30$ | $26$ | $1$ | ? | $3$ | $12$ | $28$ |" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(3,2)$ into our table, we get a function.\n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $30$ | $26$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ | $28$ |\n\nNotice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(4,12)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. \n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $30$ | $26$ | $1$ | $12$ | $3$ | $12$ | $28$ |\n\nNotice that the first row contains the value $x=4$ twice. The input value $4$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($3$ and $12$)."} :step/id #uuid "6bc8b0db-4843-5d1a-9bdd-1c1077ab9b40" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510499}}] :activity/title "Visual Representations of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509677}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106814} :activity/id #uuid "5616fc0b-edd6-562d-b17b-956a73aff3f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050802 :topic/id #uuid "68088430-21e6-5111-bf94-00596620d05f" :topic/math-academy-id 1331} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107037 :tutorial/content "The **power of product rule** states that if we want to raise the product $xy$ to some power $n$, we must raise each factor to the power $n$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n(xy)^n=x^n y^n\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n(xy)^4=x^4y^4.\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way, changing the order of factors in the multiplication. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(xy)^{4} & = \\\\ (xy)(xy)(xy)(xy) & = \\\\ (x\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x)(y\\cdot y\\cdot y\\cdot y) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of product rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "7aabbc0f-a3ca-5f05-b6ae-d7551b16ae1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1482 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "139aab34-d09f-505c-96f5-208298444874" :step/math-academy-id 3541 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105441 :question/id #uuid "ac7cb5c7-c6f1-5418-92ea-025b88681439" :question/math-academy-id "e-1081"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b4ce383-37b4-5d5a-80ef-3886605bd56f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051009}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553531}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Power of Product Rule with a Positive Power"} :step/id #uuid "7305de37-518a-5a4e-903e-40552a4f9962" :step/math-academy-id 3542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106816}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106816 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105442 :question/id #uuid "c5803e29-de29-5394-8eb7-9a0c3beea3f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1082"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85159201-5cf2-53da-91bf-ba7ef462a4fa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050208}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499514}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Power of Product Rule with a Negative Power"} :step/id #uuid "196b5dd3-ef4e-520a-aadd-9e763e63db72" :step/math-academy-id 3545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106817}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105852 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105443 :question/id #uuid "8a0ae6fe-6ba8-58bb-8835-a3e1546b0999" :question/math-academy-id "e-1083"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae5f5e65-7642-5ca1-bf84-013dbd3c7ae4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499517}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Power of Product Rule with Three Factors"} :step/id #uuid "b5b6259b-d42d-5bce-bbd7-530a36ded592" :step/math-academy-id 3548}] :activity/title "The Power of Product Rule With Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105977}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499631} :activity/id #uuid "da36029c-d9f2-59ef-b122-0fe6af0f1259" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050802 :topic/id #uuid "68088430-21e6-5111-bf94-00596620d05f" :topic/math-academy-id 1331} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ea1924-7617-55bc-a41d-f3f63731e352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{v}^{2/5}}{{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498743} {:answer/id #uuid "18c1e186-f60e-5c4e-8d46-814f50c709af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{27}^{-1}{u}^{-4}{v}^{2/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498782} {:answer/id #uuid "6f4d5935-7560-51ee-b28a-e74d36717445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27{v}^{2/5}}{{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498977} {:answer/id #uuid "98eaecfe-aa7c-56ba-90d9-9617e401d57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{9{u}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499056} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1a011f-e1bb-5e3f-807a-a03812cc1855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{{9}^{-1}{u}^{-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499056} :answer-field/id #uuid "f70e860c-0687-51a2-b8e1-d1ade86eee91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "86ce32ac-6d75-5ef7-8ed3-9dc3f527e973" :question/math-academy-id "q-3928" :question/problem "${(27{u}^{6}{v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(27{u}^{6}{v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3} & = \\\\ {({3}^{3}{u}^{6}{v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3} & = \\\\ {({3}^{3})}^{-2/3}\\cdot {({u}^{6})}^{-2/3}\\cdot {({v}^{-3/5})}^{-2/3} & = \\\\ {3}^{3\\cdot (-2/3)}\\cdot {u}^{6\\cdot (-2/3)}\\cdot {v}^{(-3/5)\\cdot (-2/3)} & = \\\\ {3}^{-6/3}\\cdot {u}^{-12/3}\\cdot {v}^{6/15} & = \\\\ {3}^{-2}\\cdot {u}^{-4}\\cdot {v}^{2/5} & = \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{{3}^{2}{u}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2/5}}{9{u}^{4}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ac43d0d9-d00d-529e-b6d7-32ae72bc24d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190ab1b2-874e-5bb3-a0ed-b02168022ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498784} {:answer/id #uuid "56bb8639-a83b-5c9f-bd37-2d2900c18393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498927} {:answer/id #uuid "5d92a4cf-c350-5c03-8652-d25b59a9ea08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49{x}^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498944} {:answer/id #uuid "a35a3d2b-2936-57a9-91bf-b88b9ea4ee2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{{x}^{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499083} {:answer/id #uuid "de2b212e-5da9-515f-8bd1-2141c6761d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{{x}^{10}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499083} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcd19327-ff40-5dae-b2cf-088be8eeca6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "997d06ae-bfea-5bdd-ad34-25a8461aee43" :question/math-academy-id "q-72553" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${(7{x}^{-5})}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(7{x}^{-5})}^{2} & = \\\\ {7}^{2}{({x}^{-5})}^{2} & = \\\\ 49\\cdot {x}^{-5\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ 49{x}^{-10} \\\\ \\frac{49}{{x}^{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b7a760a1-c69c-5443-b629-d0a8e1e4e722"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499631 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a0d9e9-f16f-5e35-91eb-3bd865760ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-{x}^{8}{y}^{-3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498733} {:answer/id #uuid "45914d4a-b895-5bc8-a1de-5a3d97104256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}{y}^{-3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498891} {:answer/id #uuid "49c8eea6-1485-5196-a051-14e2a91ed557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}{y}^{-3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498901} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a6ab37-02d3-5559-97f9-826e50636e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{8}{y}^{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499201} {:answer/id #uuid "e19915ad-81b9-58ed-9cd8-716103a6d691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-{x}^{6}{y}^{-3}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498901} :answer-field/id #uuid "010a4b8c-41ba-5ff0-99c3-e6f5f3685048" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d191fc2-9ac2-5981-97ca-4426376145b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-816" :question/problem "${(2{x}^{-2}y)}^{-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{-2}y)^{-3} & = \\\\ {2}^{-3}{({x}^{-2})}^{-3}{({y}^{1})}^{-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}\\cdot {x}^{(-2)(-3)}\\cdot {y}^{(1)(-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {x}^{6}\\cdot {y}^{-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{6}{y}^{-3}}{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb000f88-82e2-5c2e-9847-f2d22f96de08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499611}}] :activity/title "The Power of Product Rule With Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498729}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065287} :activity/id #uuid "eb9087bf-30e8-5a71-8438-1b9d7e3953ca" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029 :topic/id #uuid "7b7f598a-21a7-593a-99a2-ef8e0258c820" :topic/math-academy-id 323} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065421 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to simplify the following:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe expression above is a fraction, but there are additional fractions in the numerator and denominator.\nSuch expressions are called **fractions of fractions**.\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nThe trick to working with fractions of fractions is to remember that a fraction represents a division. So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)} = \\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) \\boldsymbol{\\div} \\left(\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{4}}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{\\color{red}{4}}{\\color{blue}{3}}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{2}{3})÷(\\frac{3}{4}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{2}{3})\\times (\\frac{4}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\times 4}{3\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{9} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)} = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5da17ec4-93cc-5398-bbfc-d50405cd2fad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8182 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "000dfe16-b4b5-5050-b07d-e01d48c6261b" :step/math-academy-id 79637 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065288}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064360 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063947 :question/id #uuid "78675372-1e29-5088-8907-52d9e54124e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13083"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68cd4840-bb77-5065-884a-ce1c8c309cd0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048392}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491190}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Fraction of Fractions"} :step/id #uuid "d17d6b7d-6849-50e4-a34b-78c672570998" :step/math-academy-id 79638 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065422 :tutorial/content "How do we simplify $\\dfrac{5}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)}?$ Notice that, this time, we have a counting number divided by a fraction.\n\nThe trick is to write $5 = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{5}}{\\color{red}{1}}$ and perform the same process as before:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{(\\frac{2}{3})} & = \\\\ 5÷(\\frac{2}{3}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{1})÷(\\frac{2}{3}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{1})\\times (\\frac{3}{2}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 3}{1\\times 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "80cee0a8-050d-53ca-8a81-4b6e37f71cdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8183 :tutorial/title "Dividing a Counting Number by a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "e39488bb-4bd7-5dfe-b572-7901ceb4d1c7" :step/math-academy-id 79642 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065290}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064361 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063948 :question/id #uuid "3bad12f4-14b2-5ab6-a1db-9d460b9a26ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-13084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ca49b4e-a4fa-5a67-9334-12e8656b5d1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553553}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Counting Number by a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "450d83cb-4e1d-5817-81f1-d89746298ceb" :step/math-academy-id 79643 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065423 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to simplify a fraction of fractions like $\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)}{4}.$ In this case, we have a fraction divided by a counting number. We have two methods available.\n\nThe long way is to write $4 = \\dfrac 4 1$ and then calculate in the same way as we did earlier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{4} & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3})÷4 & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3})÷(\\frac{4}{1}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5}{3})\\times (\\frac{1}{4}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 1}{3\\times 4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{12} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA quicker way is to recognize that the result will have the same numerator as the top fraction and that the denominator is just the product of the two denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{3})}{4}=\\frac{5}{3\\times 4}=\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03013ab2-d0a1-57fa-817f-9dd124de63f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8185 :tutorial/title "Dividing a Fraction by a Counting Number"} :step/id #uuid "05b3f520-be5d-5978-b74c-77dab033ac85" :step/math-academy-id 79647 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064362 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063949 :question/id #uuid "87974d27-c494-5b1a-84e9-03e39f3e0b1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13086"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56936b9b-2b52-58a7-ae3f-7d51492b8247" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491217}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Fraction by a Counting Number"} :step/id #uuid "8697e561-0d74-5f5b-821a-7c762a1d34a2" :step/math-academy-id 79648}] :activity/title "Fractions of Fractions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064415}]}
